course: dictionary
lesson: 15831
title: Undermine: weaken little by little
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To undermine something is to make it weaker, little by little, often in a secret way. Undermining someone makes them feel less confident or less respected. You can find the meaning by reading the clues around the word. «Mia's classmates spread nasty gossip, which slowly undermined her confidence.» Spreading gossip is unkind, and her confidence is getting weaker, so undermine means to weaken.
explain: Undermine can also mean to dig under something, like a wall or a cliff, so that it may fall. «The waves undermined the cliff, and a huge rock crashed onto the beach.» The sea is working away at the bottom of the cliff, and then the rock falls. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Try weaken in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Look for the clues. If someone is being teased, gossiped about, or ignored, the word undermine may tell you that their confidence is being worn down. If the text is about rocks, water, or the ground, undermine probably means wearing away the bottom so the thing may fall.
fact: Undermine is a verb: undermine, undermines, undermined, undermining.
fact: To undermine something is to make it weaker little by little, often secretly.
fact: To undermine someone is to make them less confident or less respected.
fact: To undermine can also mean to dig under something, such as a wall or cliff, so that it may fall.
fact: Useful phrases: «undermine confidence» and «undermine authority».
exercise: «Teasing and nasty gossip can undermine a friend's confidence.» What does undermine mean here? | *Make someone less confident | Paint a wall bright blue | Count the words in a story | Make someone very happy
exercise: «The sea slowly undermined the cliff, and a huge rock crashed onto the beach.» What does undermined mean here? | Made the cliff taller | Painted the cliff bright blue | *Wore away the base of the cliff so it could fall | Climbed to the top of the cliff
exercise: «The rude comments began to undermine the captain's authority.» Which word is closest in meaning to undermine? | Celebrate | Praise | Forget | *Weaken
test: «Lies spread about her slowly undermined her respect in the class.» What does undermined mean? | *Made weaker | Made bigger | Made louder
test: «Each small crack let the water undermine the old wall.» What does undermine mean in this sentence? | Build up under | *Dig away underneath, so it may fall | Lift higher | Paint over
exam: Which sentence uses undermine to mean make someone less confident or respected? | *The mean comments undermined her friend's confidence | The rain washed the muddy road | The builders painted the new bridge red | The children sang a song in the hall
